title: Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma and Waldenström macroglobulinemia
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

* ''Core distinction:'' Waldenström macroglobulinemia (WM) is marrow lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma (LPL) with circulating monoclonal IgM.
** Non-IgM, nonsecretory, or exclusively extramedullary LPL is not WM.

!! Diagnosis and differential

* ''LPL histology:'' Small B lymphocytes, plasmacytoid lymphocytes, and plasma cells; exclude another defined lymphoma.
** Marrow aspirate/trephine biopsy: morphology, immunohistochemistry, and flow cytometry demonstrating shared light-chain restriction.
** B cells: CD19+, CD20+, surface IgM+, CD25+; usually CD5−, CD10−, CD23−.
*** Occasional CD5/CD10/CD23 expression does not exclude LPL; plasma cells express CD38/CD138.
* ''WM threshold:'' Monoclonal IgM of any concentration plus convincing clonal marrow lymphoplasmacytic infiltration.
** Gertz/Mayo criteria require ≥10%; LYSA/ICC/IWWM accept characteristic aggregates below 10%.
** Tiny flow-detected clones alone are insufficient; document which diagnostic framework is used.
* ''MYD88 L265P:'' Present in >90% of WM; supportive, neither required nor specific.
** Also occurs in IgM MGUS and some other lymphomas; cannot distinguish MGUS from WM.
** A negative result warrants assay-sensitivity review, broader MYD88 sequencing, and reconsideration of mimics.
* ''IgM [[myeloma|Multiple myeloma]]:'' Plasma-cell neoplasm with myeloma-defining events; favor lytic lesions, t(11;14)/cyclin D1, wild-type MYD88.
** CD138 positivity alone cannot discriminate: WM also contains clonal plasma cells.
* ''Other lymphomas:'' Marginal zone lymphoma may closely overlap; integrate tissue architecture, distribution, phenotype, and genetics.
** CLL/SLL favors CD5/CD23 coexpression; [[Mantle cell lymphoma]] favors cyclin D1/SOX11 and t(11;14).
* ''Clinical categories:'' Smoldering WM lacks attributable symptoms/end-organ injury; symptomatic WM has these features.
** IgM-mediated neuropathy, cryoglobulinemia, cold hemolysis, or amyloidosis can occur with a small clone.
** Such IgM-related disorders do not automatically establish WM.

!! Risk stratification

* ''MSS-WM:'' Modified Staging System estimates overall survival in previously untreated, symptomatic WM.

|!Variable|!Points|
|Age ≤65 years|0|
|Age 66–75 years|1|
|Age >75 years|2|
|Albumin <3.5 g/dL (35 g/L)|1|
|LDH above laboratory upper limit|2|

* ''Note'': Gertz Table 3 reports reports 1 point for LDH, ''a discrepancy from the original study (reporting error?)''.

|!Total score|!Risk group|!5-year overall survival|
|0|Low|93%|
|1|Low-intermediate|82%|
|2|Intermediate|69%|
|≥3|High|55%|

* ''Asymptomatic progression:'' Assess marrow burden, IgM, β2-microglobulin, and albumin separately.
** Gertz highlights marrow ≥70%, IgM >4,500 mg/dL, β2-microglobulin >4 mg/L, albumin <3.5 g/dL.

!! Initial work-up

* ''Clinical:'' Assess performance status, comorbidities, family history, constitutional symptoms, bleeding, vision, neuropathy, cold-induced symptoms.
** Examine lymph nodes, liver/spleen, skin, peripheral nerves, and fundi.
* ''Blood:'' CBC/differential, smear, renal/liver chemistry, calcium, albumin, LDH, β2-microglobulin; include uric acid.
* ''Paraprotein:'' Serum electrophoresis/immunofixation and quantitative IgM, IgG, IgA.
** Kapoor/Gertz include serum free light chains and 24-hour urine electrophoresis/immunofixation.
** LYSA reserves free light chains for suspected amyloidosis or monoclonal-protein renal disease.
* ''Marrow/genetics:'' Aspirate plus trephine, immunophenotyping, and sensitive marrow MYD88 L265P PCR.
** Peripheral-blood testing can be falsely negative; flow may underestimate marrow infiltration.
** LYSA recommends diagnostic karyotype and FISH for del(6q)/trisomy 4.
** Expanded profile: CXCR4 sequencing, TP53 sequencing, del(17p) FISH; consider CD19-enriched material.
*** LYSA places mandatory TP53/del(17p) assessment in the pre-first-treatment work-up, not diagnostic criteria.
* ''Imaging:'' CT chest/abdomen/pelvis ± neck assesses adenopathy and organomegaly.
** PET/CT particularly when transformation is suspected; biopsy suspicious tissue.
** Routine skeletal survey is unnecessary; investigate bone symptoms or suspected myeloma.
* ''Hyperviscosity:'' Check serum viscosity with suggestive symptoms or IgM >4,000 mg/dL (Gertz).
** Retinal examination with suspected hyperviscosity or IgM ≥3,000 mg/dL.
* ''Targeted tests:'' Select according to presentation, rather than ordering every complication panel.
** Hemolysis: reticulocytes, bilirubin, haptoglobin, direct antiglobulin test, cold agglutinins.
** Cold/vasculitic symptoms: cryoglobulins/cryocrit; ensure warm specimen handling; assess hepatitis C.
** Bleeding: coagulation studies, von Willebrand antigen/activity, factor VIII.
** Neuropathy: neurology assessment, nerve conduction/electromyography, anti-MAG (myelin-associated glycoprotein) antibodies.
** Suspected amyloidosis: Congo-red tissue testing with typing; urine protein, cardiac biomarkers/echocardiography as indicated.
** CNS symptoms: MRI and cerebrospinal-fluid evaluation for Bing–Neel syndrome.
** Baseline infection assessment: hepatitis B/C and HIV as appropriate.

